1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:02,620
So, um, imagine sitting in your ministry, right?

2
00:00:02,920 --> 00:00:08,240
Looking at your program, and you're answering to your minister about, say, a new national scholarship launch.

3
00:00:08,600 --> 00:00:10,960
Right. And your deadline is in, what, six months.

4
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:17,680
Exactly. So the fastest way to get it done is, well, you build your own custom identity check and your own payment links.

5
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:22,160
Which is unfortunately how you fall into the most expensive trap in government IT.

6
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:30,880
Yeah. So today on this deep dive, we're unpacking a concept that really should be the cornerstone of your next build, which is that reuse is the return on planning.

7
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:38,800
Yeah. We're exploring why agencies build redundant tech systems, and, uh, how utilizing shared blocks fundamentally solves this problem.

8
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:43,740
Because the root cause of this trap is actually how government funding timelines operate.

9
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:48,160
Right. Projects are funded to deliver their own specific service on a specific date.

10
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:52,060
And that rigid timeline directly incentivizes isolated builds.

11
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:55,120
You know, you need to hit your deadline, so you just build a silo.

12
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:59,840
Yeah. And as a result, the systems you end up with are fragmented.

13
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:05,220
They become siloed, duplicated, and, um, incredibly costly to change.

14
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:09,720
With the same data rekeyed into systems that are completely locked into one supplier.

15
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,540
Exactly. But wait, I wanna push back on that for a second.

16
00:01:13,020 --> 00:01:15,040
If I'm running the scholarship service,

17
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:20,560
why would I risk my tight deadline relying on some external system I don't even control?

18
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:27,320
Well- I mean, if it's so clearly more expensive for the whole government to build custom systems, why does the saving go unclaimed so often?

19
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:31,620
Because that saving doesn't exist on your individual ledger.

20
00:01:32,260 --> 00:01:32,340
It,

21
00:01:33,640 --> 00:01:33,780
um,

22
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,980
it really comes down to structural reality.

23
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:37,840
Right.

24
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:44,560
The first ministry pays to build a shared block, and then the second, third, and fourth reuse it.

25
00:01:44,840 --> 00:01:45,260
Oh, I see.

26
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:51,180
So that financial sum, the actual saving, only exists at the level of the whole government.

27
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:57,540
So if the structural reality is that ministries are incentivized to build in isolation, how do we actually break that cycle?

28
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:01,960
We can look at a demonstration country that figured this out, actually: Progessa.

29
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:08,720
Okay, so if a service in Progessa, like a scholarship payment, tried to build this alone, what would that actually look like for them?

30
00:02:09,639 --> 00:02:11,580
The administrative burden would be massive.

31
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:19,360
Alone, the scholarship service needs to build and, uh, maintain links to every single bank and mobile money provider in the country.

32
00:02:19,540 --> 00:02:25,840
Oh, wow. Yeah. And it has to capture every learner's account details and maintain them securely in its own register.

33
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,940
But instead, Progessa uses the payments block.

34
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:34,220
Exactly. By reusing the payments block, one central account mapper routes the money.

35
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:38,700
So it keeps those sensitive payment details completely out of the scholarship register.

36
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:39,020
Right.

37
00:02:39,640 --> 00:02:44,700
The complexity is handled once. And it's the exact same story for learner registration.

38
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:48,120
Because they reuse the identity block for a single sign-in.

39
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:58,240
Yeah. So the registration service entirely bypasses the need to build its own credentials, manage its own cybersecurity, or, you know, hire its own password reset support

40
00:02:58,300 --> 00:02:58,620
staff.

41
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:02,860
And these blocks are highly adaptable to specific sectors like education.

42
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:08,980
Oh, absolutely. You can use that same payment block for paying school fees or redeeming school-only vouchers.

43
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:12,440
Or combining several registrations into one single service.

44
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:12,700
Right.

45
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:17,220
But to make this work, planners really rely on two core concepts.

46
00:03:17,940 --> 00:03:21,160
Which are the reference architecture and the once only principle.

47
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:24,180
Yeah. So how do those actually function mechanically?

48
00:03:24,800 --> 00:03:30,500
Well, the once only principle dictates that citizens should only have to provide their information to the government one time.

49
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:31,420
Makes sense.

50
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:40,220
And to achieve that, planners use the reference architecture, which asks them to actively identify shared services and data right from the start.

51
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:49,380
That makes perfect sense in theory, but I mean in the reality of government, you have policymakers and engineers speaking two completely different languages.

52
00:03:49,460 --> 00:03:49,940
Right, they do.

53
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:54,480
So how do you actually get the business side and IT to agree on the structure?

54
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:57,140
With a surprisingly simple tool, actually.

55
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:02,160
They use a table, mapping which specific services reuse which block.

56
00:04:02,660 --> 00:04:03,760
Oh, just a simple table.

57
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:04,080
Yeah.

58
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:09,740
It gives both the policy side and the technology side one shared language for that decision.

59
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:14,300
Which bridges the gap between the abstract architecture and concrete administration.

60
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:23,120
Exactly. So the core takeaway today for anyone running these systems is this: two services reusing two blocks creates a very real saving.

61
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:25,880
But you have to elevate your perspective.

62
00:04:25,940 --> 00:04:32,720
Right. That return on planning is only visible to someone who plans for the whole government, rather than looking strictly at their own isolated mandate.

63
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:39,600
Because we so often treat these issues as technological hurdles, when in reality, they're organizational challenges.

64
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:42,900
The technology for shared blocks has existed for years.

